          <li>Lead generation tactics that work in Malaysia</li>
          <li>A simple action plan for this week and this month</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <p class="sub" style="margin-top: 40px;">Based on real projects and current market patterns in Malaysia · 2026</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-break"></div>

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <p>
      This guide is for Malaysian SME owners and managers who want straight answers: <strong>what does a website
      actually cost, and how do I get enquiries from it?</strong>
    </p>
    <p>You'll get:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Real website pricing ranges</strong> — one-time, monthly, and web app pricing in RM.</li>
      <li><strong>What actually drives leads</strong> — the difference between a "nice website" and a "working website".</li>
      <li><strong>Simple actions you can take this week</strong> — no jargon, no big budgets.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The numbers and tactics here are based on real projects and current market patterns in Malaysia as of 2026.
    </p>

    <div class="page-break"></div>

    <!-- Part 1: Pricing -->
    <h2>Part 1 — Website Pricing in Malaysia (2026)</h2>

    <h3>One-time website packages</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One-page business website</strong> — roughly <span class="tag">RM1,500–2,500</span>. Hero, services, contact, WhatsApp button. Fits most freelancers, clinics, salons, and service businesses.</li>
      <li><strong>Multi-page business website</strong> — roughly <span class="tag">RM3,000–7,000</span>. Services, team, testimonials, blog, individual pages.</li>
      <li><strong>Company profile (PDF vs website)</strong> — a designed PDF profile is cheaper (hundreds of RM) and faster; a dedicated profile website is usually RM2,000+, but doubles as a sales tool that can be shared and updated.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Managed / monthly website plans</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Managed maintenance plans</strong> — from <span class="tag">RM199/mo</span>. Covers updates, small content changes, backup and uptime. Cheaper than buying an ad-hoc "edit" every time you need a change.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Web apps &amp; systems</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Dashboards, order systems, customer portals, automation</strong> — from <span class="tag">RM5,000+</span>. Priced per project based on scope, never blindly per hour.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What actually affects the price</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Number of pages</strong> — more pages, more design and content work.</li>
      <li><strong>Design complexity</strong> — custom branding, animations, and bespoke layouts cost more than clean templates.</li>
      <li><strong>Content readiness</strong> — you supply text/images = lower cost; you ask them to write it = higher cost and slower launch.</li>
      <li><strong>Bilingual / multilingual</strong> — Bahasa Melayu + English (or Chinese/Tamil) adds work at every step.</li>
      <li><strong>Functionality</strong> — forms, booking, payment, admin login, integrations. Each adds real scope.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="box">
      <h3>Quick cost table</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> One-page website — RM1,500–2,500</li>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> Multi-page business website — RM3,000–7,000</li>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> Managed monthly plan — from RM199/mo</li>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> Company profile PDF — few hundred RM; as a website — RM2,000+</li>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> Web app / dashboard / system — RM5,000+</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Common mistakes when buying a website</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Picking the cheapest quote — rework and hidden add-ons usually cost more in the end.</li>
      <li>No written scope — then "revisions" mean extras.</li>
      <li>No content prepared — the site sits in a holding pattern for weeks.</li>
      <li>Design-first, no consideration of how it converts — pretty but generates zero enquiries.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="page-break"></div>

    <!-- Part 2: Leads -->
    <h2>Part 2 — What Makes a Website Actually Generate Leads</h2>

    <p>
      Most SME websites get almost no enquiries. Not because they're ugly — because they're built as brochures,
      not as lead-generation tools.
    </p>

    <h3>The difference between a "nice website" and a "working website"</h3>
    <p>
      A nice website looks good and describes you. A working website tells a visitor <strong>exactly what to do
      next, in seconds</strong> — and makes doing it frictionless.
    </p>

    <h3>Key elements that matter</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Clear offer</strong> — a visitor should understand what you do and who it's for within 5 seconds of landing.</li>
      <li><strong>Strong WhatsApp CTA</strong> — a persistent "Chat on WhatsApp" button above the fold, because most of your traffic reads on their phone.</li>
      <li><strong>Fast loading on mobile</strong> — slow websites lose enquiries; mobile visitors are most of your traffic.</li>
      <li><strong>Focused landing pages vs a busy homepage</strong> — a simple page per offer (e.g. "website packages" vs "dashboard project") converts far better than one crowded homepage.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="box">
      <h3>Checklist — does your current website have these?</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> Clear offer in the first screen</li>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> WhatsApp button above the fold, on mobile too</li>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> Loads fast on a phone (test on 4G)</li>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> Each main offer has its own focused page or section</li>
        <li><span class="check">▸</span> Someone (or a form) actually replies within the hour</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Miss more than one? Your website is costing you leads even if it looks great.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-break"></div>

    <!-- Part 3: Tactics -->
    <h2>Part 3 — Lead Generation Tactics That Work in Malaysia</h2>

    <h3>The big shift: WhatsApp is now the main first-contact channel</h3>
    <p>
      For most Malaysian customers, WhatsApp is the fastest and most natural way to reach a business — and most
      SME enquiries now start there. If your website makes it easy to click-to-WhatsApp and you reply fast, you're
      already ahead of most competitors.
    </p>

    <div class="quote">
      "Reply speed is a lead-gen tactic. Businesses that reply within an hour win the enquiry."
    </div>

    <h3>Reply speed and why it matters</h3>
    <p>
      The faster you reply, the more enquires turn into customers. A slow reply (hours or next day) means the
      customer has already contacted two other businesses. Aim to reply <strong>within the hour, during business
      hours</strong>.
    </p>

    <h3>5–7 highest-impact tactics</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Google Business Profile</strong> — free, and most local searches surface it. Keep it updated, respond to reviews, post photos.</li>
      <li><strong>Click-to-WhatsApp everywhere</strong> — on your website, Google profile, social bios, even your printed materials.</li>
      <li><strong>Focused landing pages</strong> — one page per offer (service, package, or product) instead of a busy homepage.</li>
      <li><strong>Useful content / guides</strong> — short practical posts answer the questions customers actually search, and build trust.</li>
      <li><strong>A fast follow-up system</strong> — set a rule: every enquiry gets a reply today. Even "thanks, let me check and come back to you" beats silence.</li>
      <li><strong>Simple lead magnets</strong> — a short PDF (like this one) or a checklist traded for an email, so visitors who aren't ready to buy today stay on your list.</li>
      <li><strong>(Optional) basic paid ads</strong> — Google or Meta ads directed at a focused landing page (not your homepage) can work, but only once the basics above are solid.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Use both WhatsApp and email properly</h3>
    <p>
      WhatsApp is for fast conversations. Email is for ownership — a list you control where you can send updates,
      notes, and offers even if a platform changes. A simple "get practical notes" form on your site turns
      one-time visitors into repeat customers over months.
    </p>

    <div class="page-break"></div>

    <!-- Part 4: Action plan -->
    <h2>Part 4 — A Simple Action Plan</h2>

    <h3>This week (30 min total)</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Add a click-to-WhatsApp button to your website home page (and mobile).</li>
      <li>Update your Google Business Profile — hours, photos, and a link to WhatsApp.</li>
      <li>Write down your one-sentence offer ("We do X for Y customers"). If you can't, neither can your visitors.</li>
      <li>Set a reply-time rule for enquiries: within the hour, during business hours.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>This month</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Build one focused landing page for your main offer (services, price, WhatsApp CTA, at least 2 testimonials).</li>
      <li>Start collecting emails with a simple form — "get practical notes" or a PDF guide.</li>
      <li>Publish 2 short practical posts or guides that answer your customers' real questions.</li>
      <li>Review what your current website does on mobile with real 4G — slow? Fix it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Quick self-audit questions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Can a visitor tell what I do in 5 seconds?</li>
      <li>Is the WhatsApp button impossible to miss on a phone?</li>
      <li>Do I reply to enquiries within the hour?</li>
      <li>Am I collecting emails, or losing visitors after they leave?</li>
    </ul>

